1. What is React Rendering?

Rendering means showing something on the screen.

When React creates or updates the webpage, it is called rendering.

Example
function App() {
  return <h1>Hello React</h1>;
}

React displays

Hello React

That process is called rendering.





2. Initial Render

Initial Render means

The very first time React displays your app.

Example

When you open a website

Website opens
       ↓
React starts
       ↓
Creates UI
       ↓
Shows page

Like opening YouTube.

When you first open it, all videos appear.

That is the Initial Render




3. Re-rendering

Re-render means

React updates the screen after something changes.

Example

Initially

Count: 0

After clicking a button

Count: 1

React redraws only the updated part.

That is called Re-rendering.




Real DOM

DOM = Document Object Model

It is the browser's representation of your webpage.

Example

<html>
  <body>
      <h1>Hello</h1>
      <button>Click</button>
  </body>
</html>

Browser creates

Document

├── Body
     ├── H1
     └── Button

This is called the Real DOM.

The browser actually displays it.



5. Virtual DOM

React creates its own copy of the Real DOM.

This copy is called the Virtual DOM.

Think of it as a rough draft before making changes to the real page.

Example

Real DOM

Count = 5

React creates

Virtual DOM

Count = 6

React compares the old and new Virtual DOMs.

If only the count changed, React updates only that part.


6. Render Cycle

Render Cycle means

The complete process React follows whenever something changes.

Flow

Something changes
        ↓
Component runs again
        ↓
New Virtual DOM created
        ↓
Compare with old Virtual DOM
        ↓
Find differences
        ↓
Update Real DOM
        ↓
Browser shows new UI

Example

Button Click
      ↓
State changes
      ↓
Component runs
      ↓
React compares
      ↓
Only changed text updates



7. React Updates UI Automatically

In normal JavaScript, you usually update the page manually.

Example

document.getElementById("count").innerText = count;

In React

setCount(count + 1);

That's it.

React automatically updates the screen.

You don't need to manually change the HTML.


8. Keys (Introduction)

Keys are unique IDs for list items.

Example

const fruits = ["Apple", "Mango", "Orange"];

Without keys

React gets confused about which item changed.

With keys

<li key={1}>Apple</li>
<li key={2}>Mango</li>
<li key={3}>Orange</li>

React quickly knows

Which item is new
Which item was removed
Which item changed

You'll learn keys in detail later.



