9. Component Rendering Flow (Simple Explanation)

A component is like a function that returns UI.

Example

function Welcome() {
  return <h1>Hello</h1>;
}

React follows these steps whenever it needs to display or update a component.

Step 1: Component is called
Welcome();

React executes the component function.

Welcome()

⬇

Step 2: JSX is created
return <h1>Hello</h1>;

React receives the JSX.

<h1>Hello</h1>

⬇

Step 3: Virtual DOM is created

React stores a virtual version of the UI.

Virtual DOM

H1
└── Hello

⬇

Step 4: Compare with the previous Virtual DOM

If this is the first render:

No previous Virtual DOM

React simply displays it.

If it's a re-render:

Old Virtual DOM
       ↓
Compare
       ↓
New Virtual DOM

⬇

Step 5: Update the Real DOM

Only the changed parts are updated.

Real DOM updated

⬇

Step 6: Browser displays the updated UI

You see the latest content on the screen.

Complete Component Rendering Flow
Component Function
        │
        ▼
Runs
        │
        ▼
Returns JSX
        │
        ▼
Create Virtual DOM
        │
        ▼
Compare with Old Virtual DOM
        │
        ▼
Find Changes
        │
        ▼
Update Real DOM
        │
        ▼
Browser Displays Updated UI
🎯 Real-World Analogy

Imagine a teacher correcting a student's essay.

The student writes a new draft (Virtual DOM).
The teacher compares it with the old draft.
The teacher marks only the changed sentences, not the whole essay.
The final corrected essay is printed (Real DOM).

React works in a very similar way—it compares the old and new versions and updates only what changed instead of rebuilding the entire page.

